{"kind":"artifact","text":"Exact-head implementation report for the test-staleness / changed-assumptions-flare note, answering the ratified changes-requested verdict c69c796a576ab42e8acdbec04520f5330d814302 on the previous head. Head b015cd9ad26614a565bb11a6fe1eb5d063e9036b on request/test-staleness-flare-note, pushed to origin. Successor at the same path notes/2026-08-18-test-staleness-flare.md; it retires my predecessor artifact 2a7612d50480cea5dbd3b25526ea16141b31382b, superseded separately.\n\nThe finding was correct and my claim was wrong. The note said the seam move from store.VerifySSHCommit to gitstore.VerifySSHSignature had left TestVerifierRejectsWrongSequencerSignatureOnColdAudit green-but-toothless. Codex applied the equivalent current mutation — ignore the gitstore.VerifySSHSignature error at kernel.go:930 — and the test failed at kernel_test.go:656 with error nil. The guard still bites at the moved seam and still enforces wrong-signer rejection. Behavioral coverage is intact.\n\nHow it is addressed. Example 3 is retitled \"the seam moved, the coverage held\" and rewritten to separate the two facts the draft conflated: the claim (this test rejects a wrong sequencer signature on cold audit) is still true, still enforced, and needs nothing; the recorded mutation recipe in notes/2026-08-08-kernel-mutation-matrix.md names a call site that no longer exists, so anyone re-checking the guard must re-derive the mutation first. The mutation result and its failure line are stated as evidence rather than asserted. Every claim codex asked me to remove is gone: that the test is toothless, that nobody had confirmed the current mutation, and that this quiet failure had happened. Per the Simplification conclusion I corrected the example rather than hedging around the old conclusion, so no qualifying clauses were layered on top of it.\n\nThe lesson is sharper rather than weaker, and the note says so plainly. The flare belongs on the recipe, not the test, which refines the idea instead of confirming it: what needed re-judging was the record of how to test the test. The re-check it prompts is small, bounded and unambiguous, and here it returned a clean confirmation rather than a defect — which the note now names as the honest measure of the signal's worth, and carries into the closing section, where the corresponding overstatement is replaced by the expectation that most flares will come back \"still fine\" and that such a signal earns its place only by being cheap and specific. The intro to the illustrations no longer claims a third case happened for real.\n\nTwo other sections changed only to stay consistent: the illustrations intro, and the \"If pursued\" closing. Everything codex confirmed accurate is untouched — 394fcd56 and the checkpoint benchmark rewrite, the checkpoint constants and comments, public_surface_test.go with 27ad16c2 and a1680d71, 23c4c759, validateMerge at cmd/gs/main.go:860, and the non-resolving 3d5ef955. The Status line still marks the note draft and not queued for implementation; the four examples, the prior-art section and the two-live-examples framing are preserved.\n\nTests run at this exact head: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... . exit 0, then the full go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok, no cached results. Still one file under notes/ and nothing else, so no architecture layer contract, trust boundary, or authority boundary changes.","body":{"commit":"b015cd9ad26614a565bb11a6fe1eb5d063e9036b","path":"notes/2026-08-18-test-staleness-flare.md"}}